In this lesson, we'll look at average rate of change, which is really just the slope of a line between two points on a curve. Picture a smooth curve, and pick two points on it. Draw a straight line connecting them. That line is called a secant, and its slope is your average rate of change.

So how do you find that slope? It's rise over run, the same idea you've used since year seven. The change in y divided by the change in x. If you're tracking distance in metres against time in seconds, then your answer comes out in metres per second, so always carry the units through.

Now here's the cool part. As the two points get closer together, the change in x shrinks toward zero. The secant line starts to hug the curve more tightly, and the average rate of change gets closer and closer to the slope of the tangent at that point. That limiting value is the derivative, which we'll formalise later.

So the pattern is simple. Two points far apart give you an average rate of change. Two points squeezing together reveal the instantaneous rate of change. Now let's see it in action.